HST Office: Linux (GTK) builds the native menu bar only when it is shown, so spreadsheets open and close faster.

A user reported that a ~500 KB .xlsx opens slowly and that closing it lags on Linux (CaramOS = Linux Mint 22.3
Cinnamon, WildOS = Debian 13 KDE Plasma 6 on Wayland), while the same file is fine on Windows. Profiles of HST 1.0.1
(perf with DWARF call graphs, same file, same machine) show that the difference is the GTK menu bar:

- Opening: MenuBarWindow::SetMenu() -> GtkSalMenu::SetFrame() ran UpdateFull() synchronously.
  UpdateFull() activates every submenu of the Calc menu bar, which asks every command for its
  state (and the system clipboard for Paste), and GTK then creates a widget for every item
  (gtk_menu_bar_new_from_model): 1.0 s in the profile of a build with symbols, as long as the
  import itself, and 0.5-0.7 s of CPU in the release build (2.8 GHz Xeon). Then the Tabbed UI
  (the HST default) hid the menu bar, so its widget was destroyed again.
- Closing: SfxObjectShell::PrepareClose() -> SfxNotebookBar::CloseMethod() shows the menu bar
  of the closing document again (MenuBar::SetDisplayable(true)), so GTK built the whole widget
  tree (0.27 s), only to destroy it when the window switched to the Start Center a moment later.
- The VCL menu bar used on Windows, macOS and by the X11 plugin creates submenus only when they
  open, so none of this happens there.

GtkSalMenu now defers the full update and the widget to an idle (maBuildMenuBarIdle) outside
Unity mode (no global menu; in Unity mode the model is the visible menu and is built as before).
The idle builds only a menu bar that is still displayable and whose window is shown (the window
of a document is shown after the load, when the Tabbed UI has already hidden the menu bar; the
idle itself can run earlier, inside a nested main loop of the load); GtkSalFrame::Show() calls
GtkSalMenu::FrameShown(), which starts the idle again; ShowMenuBar(true) of a menu bar that was
never built starts the idle again, ShowMenuBar(false) (also from MenuBar::ImplDestroy when a
window gets another menu bar) stops it, as does DetachFromFrame() and EnableUnity(true). F10
before the idle ran builds it at once (TakeFocus). The hierarchy idle (menu customisation, and
Update() after the global-menu watch reported no registrar, which arrives while a document is
still loading) rebuilds at once only a menu bar that was already built. The menu bar of a new
document under the Tabbed UI and the one shown again for a closing document are therefore never
built; with the standard toolbars the menu bar is built at the first idle after its window is
shown.

